WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:11.650
Today, we are going to explore a paper that
improves on the incredible StyleGAN2.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:13.089
What is that?

00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:18.650
StyleGAN2 is a neural network-based learning
algorithm that is not only capable of creating

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:24.590
these eye-poppingly detailed images of human
beings that don’t even exist, but, it also

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:28.860
improved on its previous version in a number
of different ways.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:34.160
For instance, with the original StyleGAN method,
we could exert some artistic control over

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:41.510
these images, however, look, you see how this
part of the teeth and eyes are pinned to a

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:47.620
particular location and the algorithm just
refuses to let it go, sometimes to the detriment

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.310
of its surroundings.

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:55.220
The improved StyleGAN2 method addressed this
problem, you can see the results here.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:00.649
Teeth and eyes are now allowed to float around
freely, and perhaps this is the only place

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:04.251
on the internet where we can say that and
be happy about it.

00:01:04.251 --> 00:01:11.360
It could also mix two images together, and
it could do it not only for human faces, but

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.290
for cars, buildings, horses, and more.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:20.850
And get this, this paper was published in
December 2019, and since then, it has been

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:26.460
used in a number of absolutely incredible
applications and followup works.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.310
Let’s look at three of them.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:33.420
One, for instance, the first question I usually
hear when I talk about an amazing paper like

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.280
this is “okay, great, but when do I get
to use this”?

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:43.330
And the answer is, right now, because it is
implemented in Photoshop in a feature that

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.760
is called Neural Filters.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:52.860
Two, artistic control over these images has
improved so much that now, we can pin down

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:58.340
a few intuitive parameters and change them
with minimal changes to other parts of the

00:01:58.340 --> 00:01:59.340
image.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:05.420
For instance, it could grow Elon Musk a majestic
beard…and, Elon Musk was not the only person

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:11.930
who got an algorithmic beard, I hope you know
what’s coming…yes, I got one too!

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.719
Let me know in the comments whose beard you
liked better!

00:02:14.719 --> 00:02:21.269
Three, a nice followup paper that could take
a photo of Abraham Lincoln and other historic

00:02:21.269 --> 00:02:27.299
figures, and could restore their images as
if we were time travelers and took these photos

00:02:27.299 --> 00:02:29.549
with a more modern camera.

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:35.450
The best part here was that it leveraged the
superb morphing capabilities of StyleGAN2

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:41.230
and took an image of their siblings, a person
who has somewhat similar proportions to the

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:47.129
target subject, and morph them into a modern
image of this historic figure.

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:54.219
This was brilliant, because restoring images
is hard, but with StyleGAN2, morphing is now

00:02:54.219 --> 00:03:00.189
easy, so the authors decided to trade a difficult
problem for an easier one.

00:03:00.189 --> 00:03:03.510
And the results speak for themselves.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:08.680
We cannot know for sure if this is what these
historic figures really looked like, but for

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:12.840
now, it makes one heck of a thought experiment.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.690
And now, let’s marvel together at these
beautiful results with the new method, that

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:20.379
goes by the name, StyleGAN2-ADA.

00:03:20.379 --> 00:03:24.849
While we look through these results, all of
which were generated with the new method,

00:03:24.849 --> 00:03:27.170
here are three things that it does better.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:36.629
One, if often works just as well as StyleGAN2
but requires ten times fewer images for training.

00:03:36.629 --> 00:03:42.040
This means that now, it can create these beautiful
images, and this can be done by training a

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:47.580
set of neural networks from less than 10 thousand
images at a time.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:48.909
Whoa.

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:50.969
That is not much at all.

00:03:50.969 --> 00:03:54.299
Two, it creates better quality results.

00:03:54.299 --> 00:04:00.409
The baseline here is original StyleGAN2, the
numbers are subject to minimization and are

00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:03.499
a measure of the quality of these images.

00:04:03.499 --> 00:04:08.040
As you see from the bolded numbers, the new
method not only beats the baseline method

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.620
substantially, but it does it across the board.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.590
That is a rare sight indeed.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:21.840
And three, we can train this method faster,
it generates these images faster, and in the

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.210
meantime, also consumes less memory, which
is usually in short supply on our graphics

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.210
cards.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:33.800
Now, we noted that the new version of the
method is called StyleGAN2-ADA.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.610
What is ADA?

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.830
This part means Adaptive Discriminator Augmentation.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.910
What does that mean exactly?

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:46.910
This means that the new method endeavors to
squeeze as much information out of these training

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.040
datasets as it can.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.480
Data augmentation is not new, it has been
done for many years now, and essentially this

00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:01.280
means that we rotate, colorize, or even corrupt
these images during the training process.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:06.820
The key here is that with this, we are artificially
increasing the number of training samples

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:08.620
the neural network sees.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:13.550
The difference here is that they used a greater
set of augmentations, and the adaptive part

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:19.540
means that these augmentations are tailored
more to the dataset at hand.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:25.010
And now comes the best part, hold on to your
papers, and let’s look at the timeline here.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:35.130
StyleGAN2 appeared in December 2019, and StyleGAN2-ADA,
this method came out just half a year later.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.800
Such immense progress, in just 6 months of
time.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:44.310
The pace of progress in machine learning research
is absolutely stunning these days.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:49.500
Imagine what we will be able to do with these
techniques just a couple more years down the

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:50.500
line.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:51.500
What a time to be alive!

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:58.230
But this paper also teaches a very important
lesson to us that I would like to show you.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:04.880
Have a look at this table that shows the energy
expenditure for this project for transparency,

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:11.050
but it also tells us the number of experiments
that were required to finish such an amazing

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:12.390
paper.

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:16.740
And that is more than 3300 experiments.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:22.020
255 of which were wasted due to technical
problems.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:28.200
In the foreword of my PhD thesis, I wrote
the following: “Research is the study of

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.490
failure.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:34.290
More precisely, research is the study of obtaining
new knowledge through failure.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:41.990
A bad researcher fails 100% of the time, while
a good one fails only 99% of the time.

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:48.180
Hence, what you see written here (and in most
papers) is only 1% of the work that has been

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.180
done.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:55.090
I would like to thank Felícia, my wife, for
providing motivation, shielding me from distractions,

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:59.400
and bringing sunshine to my life to endure
through many of these failures.”

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:05.139
This paper is a great testament to show how
difficult the life of a researcher is.

00:07:05.139 --> 00:07:11.210
How many people give up their dreams, after
being rejected once, or maybe two times?

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:12.470
Ten times?

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:15.010
Most people give up after 10 tries.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:21.389
And just imagine having a 1000 failed experiments
and still not even being close to publishing

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:22.600
a paper yet.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:27.530
And, with a little more effort, this amazing
work came out of it.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.910
Failing doesn’t mean losing.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.730
Not in the slightest.

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:36.690
Huge congratulations to the authors for their
endurance, and for this amazing work, and

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:42.920
I think this also shows that these Weights
and Biases experiment tracking tools are invaluable,

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:48.540
because it is next to impossible to remember
what went wrong with each of them, and what

00:07:48.540 --> 00:08:38.800
should 

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:46.339
be fixed.

00:08:46.339 --> 00:08:49.419
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

